(D) Lambda_2 >= 0: from the stated lower bound on delta_S alone, or from (D2) at the good model
    Lambda_2 = (2^m H(p) - L_S) + delta_S + 4 + Lambda_1 + kappa_g. With delta_S at its stated lower bound
    -2 log m - 2 log s0 (kappa = 0, B = infinity):
      d=100 m= 20 r=10: gap=   6.33, delta_S lower bound=  -28.64, Lambda_2 at that bound =    23.54
      d=100 m=100 r= 2: gap=  50.12, delta_S lower bound= -209.29, Lambda_2 at that bound =  -113.31
      d=400 m=400 r= 3: gap= 199.73, delta_S lower bound= -811.29, Lambda_2 at that bound =  -552.53
      d= 60 m= 60 r= 2: gap=  30.12, delta_S lower bound= -127.81, Lambda_2 at that bound =   -56.25
    A negative value only shows that the stated lower bound on delta_S is too weak to give Lambda_2 >= 0.
    (D2) at pi_g (level 1, l <= d + kappa_g, R_B = H(p)) gives d + kappa_g + 2^m H(p) >= d + B_S - 4 - Lambda_1,
    i.e. Lambda_2 >= 0 always.
